WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.370
In this video, you back up a SUSE Virtualization virtual machine with CloudCasa, a backup service, and restore it after it is deleted.

00:00:09.370 --> 00:00:15.980
On the Virtual Machines page, demo-cc is running in its own namespace, also named demo-cc.

00:00:15.980 --> 00:00:23.730
Open the CloudCasa console. CloudCasa by Catalogic is a backup service for Kubernetes and its virtual machines.

00:00:23.730 --> 00:00:26.390
Sign in with your CloudCasa account.

00:00:31.740 --> 00:00:38.600
The VMs page lists the virtual machines of every connected cluster. Filter for demo-cc.

00:00:38.600 --> 00:00:45.270
Its cluster, harvester-ai, was registered earlier as demo-harvester-ai, and the CloudCasa agent runs on it.

00:00:45.270 --> 00:00:50.380
Point to the actions of demo-cc, then select Define backup.

00:00:52.490 --> 00:00:56.000
The backup panel opens with demo-cc already selected.

00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:02.780
Keep Copy to backup storage. CloudCasa snapshots the disk first, then copies its data off the cluster.

00:01:02.780 --> 00:01:06.680
Open Advanced options to choose where the copy goes,

00:01:06.680 --> 00:01:12.280
and choose CloudCasa storage, which the service provides, so you need no bucket of your own.

00:01:12.280 --> 00:01:15.780
Then pick a provider and a region for the stored copy.

00:01:21.130 --> 00:01:25.280
Select Next to go to the policy step.

00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:31.150
Keep the policy at None, so the backup runs once, on demand. A policy would run it on a schedule.

00:01:31.150 --> 00:01:34.230
Select Next again to reach the summary.

00:01:34.230 --> 00:01:38.530
In the summary, name the backup demo-cc-backup,

00:01:38.530 --> 00:01:41.640
and keep it for one day. Run now is on.

00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:46.380
Select Create and Run to start the backup now.

00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:55.410
When the job finishes, Recovery points lists a copy of demo-cc on demo-harvester-ai, kept for one day.

00:01:55.410 --> 00:02:01.890
Now simulate a loss. Back on the Virtual Machines page, open the actions of demo-cc,

00:02:01.890 --> 00:02:05.000
and select Delete from the menu.

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:10.040
The disk volume is selected too. Select Delete.

00:02:10.040 --> 00:02:15.760
Back in CloudCasa, point to the actions of the recovery point and select Restore.

00:02:17.840 --> 00:02:21.110
The restore wizard includes demo-cc from the recovery point.

00:02:21.110 --> 00:02:25.480
Select Next to choose the destination.

00:02:25.480 --> 00:02:29.710
The destination is the original cluster, demo-harvester-ai.

00:02:29.710 --> 00:02:33.580
Select Next to see the restore transforms.

00:02:33.580 --> 00:02:38.170
Leave the namespace unchanged, so the virtual machine returns to demo-cc.

00:02:38.170 --> 00:02:42.250
Then open VM options to check how the virtual machine starts.

00:02:42.250 --> 00:02:47.870
The run strategy is Halted by default, which would leave it stopped. Open the list,

00:02:47.870 --> 00:02:52.550
and choose Use strategy from backup, so demo-cc starts again as it ran before.

00:02:52.550 --> 00:02:56.080
Select Next to reach the summary.

00:02:56.080 --> 00:03:00.350
Name the restore demo-cc-restore. Run now is on.

00:03:00.350 --> 00:03:05.320
Select Save and Restore to start the restore job.

00:03:05.320 --> 00:03:13.760
When the restore job finishes, demo-cc is running again on the Virtual Machines page, with its disk restored from CloudCasa.

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:21.423
The virtual machine is back, restored from CloudCasa storage. Next, you use NetApp storage with Trident.
